  "content": {
    "id": "p21-q7-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "7",
    "prompt": "",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [
      {
        "id": "p21-q7-a",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "Let $a=3+\\sqrt{2}$. Find a polynomial with integer coefficients for which $a$ is a root.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$P(x)=x^2-6x+7$.",
          "worked": "$$\\begin{align*}a-3&=\\sqrt{2}\\\\(a-3)^2&=2\\\\a^2-6a+9&=2\\\\a^2-6a+7&=0\\end{align*}$$\nThus $P(x)=x^2-6x+7$ has integer coefficients and $P(a)=0$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 35
      },
      {
        "id": "p21-q7-b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "Prove that $3+\\sqrt{2}$ is irrational.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$3+\\sqrt{2}$ is irrational.\n\nBy the Integer Root Theorem, the only possible rational roots of $x^2-6x+7$ are $\\pm1,\\pm7$; none is a root.",
          "worked": "From part (a), $a=3+\\sqrt{2}$ is a root of $P(x)=x^2-6x+7$. This is a monic polynomial with integer coefficients, so the Integer Root Theorem gives the only possible rational roots as $\\pm1,\\pm7$.\n$$\\begin{align*}P(1)&=1-6+7=2\\\\P(-1)&=1+6+7=14\\\\P(7)&=49-42+7=14\\\\P(-7)&=49+42+7=98\\end{align*}$$\nNone is zero, so $P(x)$ has no rational roots. Therefore, $3+\\sqrt{2}$ is irrational.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 55
      }
    ]
  },
